content.thinking:
Confirmed: the `smallModFontSizeH5H6` curve applies inside `.mod-smaller-headings` (a module that shrinks headings inside content modules). So `.h5.atm-heading` inside `.mod-smaller-headings` → 14/16/18px.

Base h5/h6 = the same as h4 (`regular` curve = 16 → 18 (xsmall) → 18 (large) → 20 (xlarge)).
Inside `.mod-smaller-headings`: h5/h6 = 14 → 16 (medium) → 18 (xlarge).

Our app's `.zh-heading--5` is used for the compare dialog title ("Vergleich") — a dialog bar title, more like a smaller-heading context. Given the official smallest context is 14–18px, and our compare bar title uses 16px now (inherited), I can define:

```css
.zh-heading--5,
h5,
h6 {
  font-size: var(--zh-font-regular);
  line-height: var(--zh-line-bigger);
}
@media (min-width: 840px) { .zh-heading--5, h5, h6 { font-size: var(--zh-font-bigger-regular); line-height: var(--zh-line-base); } }
```
Hmm, this is getting into design-token subtlety. Simpler and defensible: mirror the official h5/h6 base step (same as h4: 18px → 20px at ≥840) but with line-height bigger→base... Or mirror `mod-smaller-headings` h5/h6 (14 → 16 at ≥840) since our usage is a compact dialog title.

Which is truer? Our markup uses `zh-heading--5` for the comparison view title. In the canton's design, the h5/h6 element has the same size as h4 in normal context. The variant `.mod-smaller-headings` is applied to content modules. Our compare view is a dialog, not a "content module"... 

I'll implement h5/h6 as the official base (identical to h4: 18px base, 20px ≥840, line-height medium/base), and document it with the citation. That's faithful and simple. Then "Vergleich" in the compare bar renders 18/20px instead of 16 — visually fine (it has a bold black style).

Also let me define the element selectors h5/h6 for completeness (nothing else uses them).

Let me implement in zh-base.css: extend the existing `.zh-heading--4, h4` rules to include `.zh-heading--5, h5, h6` and document the derivation in docs/design-system.md. Then verify visually the compare dialog title isn't broken, run the harness (it checks compare view rendering + contrast sweep), and commit.

Actually careful: `.zh-heading--4` in our app is used for card titles (18/20px). If I make `--5` the same size, the compare title becomes the same size as card titles. Fine.

Hmm — but hold on: is there also usage in detail.js? Let me grep for `zh-heading--5` in app code.

